WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.720
A long time ago, in a galaxy far, far away,
a legendary Sith Lord was born from the ashes

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.310
of the defeated, disgraced young Anakin Skywalker.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:13.670
Star Wars villain Darth Vader is arguably
the most famous bad guy in film history.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.570
But how well do you really know the one-time
Jedi who became the Dark Side's most powerful

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:18.570
asset?

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.820
Here's the untold story of Darth Vader.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.550
It took five men to play Vader

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.869
The name most commonly associated with Darth
Vader is James Earl Jones, who provided the

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:29.400
menacing villain with his trademark baritone
voice.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.499
But that's not Jones inside the suit.

00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:35.829
Instead, the more physically imposing David
Prowse wore the armor most of the time, with

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:39.260
fencer Bob Anderson standing in for the lightsaber
duels.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.530
And that iconic moment when he takes his helmet
off in Return of the Jedi?

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.109
That's the pasty face of veteran stage actor
Sebastian Shaw.

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:51.910
Oh, and one more guy also contributed to the
performance: sound artist Ben Burtt, who created

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:56.270
Vader's trademark panting with a microphone
and a SCUBA tank regulator.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.940
The voice was a secret

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.149
His voicework may be iconic now, but back
in the day the participation of James Earl

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:06.799
Jones was such a closely guarded secret even
the cast didn't know he was playing Darth

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:07.799
Vader.

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:11.150
So it came as a really big surprise to David
Prowse, the guy inside the suit, when he learned

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.470
his voice wasn't going to be used after all.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.530
He was ticked off, but George Lucas had a
pretty good reason for making the switch,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.579
as Prowse's voicework was so pedestrian, Carrie
Fisher called him "Darth Farmer."

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:24.959
“I’m tearing this ship apart piece by
piece until you find those tapes.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.420
Find the passengers in this vessel.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.060
I want them alive!”

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.010
And Jones kept the secret for a long time.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:35.189
Not only was he not credited on A New Hope
or The Empire Strikes Back, but he straight-up

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:39.409
denied that it was his voice in the movies,
telling anyone who asked that he thought it

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:43.210
was the work of Bond villain and 7-Up spokesman
Geoffrey Holder.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:49.240
“Whatever ‘it’ is that makes 7-Up so
refreshingly different, the other soft drinks

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.930
never had it, hahaha.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:51.930
Never will.”

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.520
Orson Welles almost played Vader

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.020
While James Earl Jones eventually fessed up
to providing the voice of Darth Vader, there

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.500
was a point in time where someone else was
considered for the role instead: Orson Welles.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.140
“Ahhhh the French champagne.”

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.960
In 2015, George Lucas revealed that he auditioned
two people for the role, but once the tapes

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.310
were in, the decision pretty much made itself,
saying,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.800
"I knew the voice had to be very, very special.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.810
And I had to make a choice—a choice that
was a tough choice, but an easy choice, really—between

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.230
Orson Welles and James Earl Jones . . . [Jones]
won ."

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.000
Good thing, too, considering Welles died in
1985, while Jones has been able to come back

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.470
to voice Darth Vader again and again for years.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.110
“I have one thing to say to people like
you…”

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.080
“...I like Star Wars too!”

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.540
Vader's helmet was an afterthought

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.909
It's hard to believe, but the iconic black
helmet Darth Vader wears throughout the series

00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:48.440
wasn't even in the original script, which
simply called for a little respirator instead.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.400
It was artist Ralph McQuarrie who originally
devised the helmet, which he intended to be

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.550
part of a space suit.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.300
But the painting was so striking that costume
designer John Mollo ran with it, incorporating

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.019
elements of traditional samurai armor and
Nazi helmets from World War II.

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:04.720
He has his own castle

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.700
If you've seen Rogue One, you might have been
surprised when the film suddenly took an unexpected

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.000
detour into Mordor.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.989
But that wasn't Barad-dûr, it was actually
Bast Castle, Darth Vader's secret getaway—sort

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:17.150
of like an evil Fortress of Solitude.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.330
Bast Castle has actually appeared before in
video games, and concept art has been floating

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.640
around since the late '70s, with Ralph McQuarrie's
designs originally slated to appear in Empire

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.640
Strikes Back.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.250
There was one minor change in Rogue One, though:
the location of Bast Castle has seemingly

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:37.610
been moved from the acid rain planet of Vjun
to the lava-drenched doomscape of Mustafar,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.890
where Anakin Skywalker had his legs lopped
off by Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.270
We're guessing real estate prices were reasonable.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.150
Darth Vader's true identity was an off-script
secret

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.330
At this point, there's probably not a single
human being on Earth who doesn't know Darth

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.330
Vader is Luke Skywalker's dad.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.330
“Wooow!

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.330
What an ending!

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.670
Who’da thought Darth Vader was Luke Skywalker’s
father?”

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.849
“Oh thank you Mr. Blow The Picture For Me!”

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:05.349
But when The Empire Strikes Back came out,
Vader's famous revelation was shocking - and

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:07.669
one of the best-kept secrets in the history
of cinema.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.299
“No...I am your father.”

00:04:12.299 --> 00:04:16.019
Knowing that the real line was going to be
overdubbed in post-production anyway by James

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.420
Earl Jones, George Lucas wrote a fake line
into the script to make sure there were no

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.420
spoilers.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.290
When David Prowse read his line in the climactic
scene, what he actually said was "Obi-Wan

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.390
killed your father."

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:34.490
“When I first saw the dialogue that said,
‘Luke, I am your father,’ I said to myself,

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:35.670
‘He’s lying.’”

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.540
The only person in the scene who actually
knew the truth was Mark Hamill, who played

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.880
Luke Skywalker, and even he was only told
minutes beforehand so he could react with

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.420
the appropriate level of anguish.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.900
“No...no...that’s not true.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.440
That’s impossible!”

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.520
The name is a clue.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.520
Or is it?

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.440
According to Lucas, the name Darth Vader has
a secret meaning: "'Darth' is a variation

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.440
of dark.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.220
And 'Vader' is a variation of father.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.830
So it's basically Dark Father."

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.440
That seems like it might be a clue to the
big secret that Vader would actually turn

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.510
out to be Luke's dad.

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:17.330
But skeptics have pointed out that the whole
"Vader is Anakin" thing wasn't added to the

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:22.010
storyline until well into the writing process
for The Empire Strikes Back, years after Vader

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.050
was first named.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.100
So is Lucas full of it?

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.780
Or did the name just turn out to be a happy
coincidence?

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.210
You be the judge.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:29.990
Vader may have been swiped from Jack Kirby

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:34.620
Luke isn't the only member of the Skywalker
family whose parentage may hold a shocking

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:35.620
secret.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.980
In a case of art imitating life, it turns
out that Darth Vader's real father might not

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.460
be George Lucas at all, but rather legendary
comic book creator Jack Kirby.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:48.120
According to the book Tales to Astonish, during
a fateful dinner meeting in the early 1970s,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.890
Kirby told Lucas about a massive space opera
he was planning called The New Gods, which

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:57.250
features a helmeted villain named Darkseid
facing off against a champion of light named

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:01.570
Orion, who turns out to be Darkseid's own
son and who struggles to overcome his family's

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.100
legacy of evil.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.100
Sound familiar?

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.460
According to Ahmet Zappa, Kirby was none too
pleased with Stars Wars, feeling that Lucas

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.580
had mined not just The New Gods for inspiration,
but all of Kirby's work.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.220
"He told my dad stuff like, 'Darth Vader was
Doctor Doom and the Force is the Source' and

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.750
that George Lucas ripped him off."

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.800
Well, you know what they say: if you're going
to steal, steal from the best.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.010
Vader had an apprentice

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.140
It may come as a surprise to learn that Darth
Vader himself had an apprentice, given that

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.400
the Sith supposedly follow a really stupid
rule limiting them to only two members at

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:33.400
a time.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.570
Not to mention the fact there has been no
mention of Vader having an apprentice in any

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:37.630
of the live-action films.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:42.300
But there's a simple explanation: she was
his apprentice before he became a bad guy.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.220
And also, she's from the cartoons.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.880
Fans of Star Wars: The Clone Wars and Star
Wars Rebels know, of course, that we're talking

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.360
about Ahsoka Tano.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.390
Nobody knows exactly what Ahsoka's final fate
is, but considering she was thrown out of

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:58.040
the Jedi order before the Purge, it's possible
she may still be alive even in present continuity.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.020
Could we see her pop up in Episode VIII?

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.110
Considering the Clone Wars character Saw Gerrera
played a major role in Rogue One, we wouldn't

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:06.110
bet against it.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.110
Thanks for watching!

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.560
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.760
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

